I wrote `README.md` in the repository root. It is the only file I created, and it starts with a level-one heading.

It covers what the project is, the requirements, installation through `install.sh`, the menu and first-run behavior, Docker, updating and uninstalling, the tool categories, the repository layout, known limitations, and the MIT license. Every claim comes from the code, and I added a notice about responsible use because many of the bundled tools are offensive.

Some things you may want to look at:

- **Uninstall and update paths don't match the installer.** The in-app "Update Hackingtool" and "Uninstall" actions use `/etc/hackingtool` and `/usr/share/doc/hackingtool`. The installer puts the tool in `/usr/share/hackingtool` with a launcher at `/usr/bin/hackingtool`. The in-app uninstall therefore leaves that install behind. I documented this in Known Limitations rather than fixing it.
- **The Docker compose file uses a prebuilt image.** `docker-compose.yml` pulls `vgpastor/hackingtool` and has no `build:` section, so it doesn't run the local `Dockerfile`. The README says so.
- **`generate_readme.py` can't run.** It reads `README_template.md`, which isn't in the repository.
- **The tool category list is a sample, not the full catalog.** The category table names a few representative tools per category, taken from each module. The menu is the complete list. I didn't open every nested `TOOLS` list to confirm exact membership.

